# Phase 3b / cross-vendor verification of the GICv3-NISV fix
#
# Captured 2026-09-19 on AWS EC2 a1.metal, eu-central-1 -- bare-metal Graviton1,
# Annapurna Labs SoC, 16x Cortex-A72 (MIDR implementer 0x41, part 0xd08),
# kernel 6.8.0-1063-aws. Instance terminated immediately after this capture.
# No instance id, account id or address is recorded here, by design: the
# 2026-07-29 run leaked its instance id into git history and needed a
# filter-branch to remove it.
#
# WHY: on 2026-07-29 the SDP's shipped startup-qemu-virt hung here exactly as it
# did on the Jetson Orin Nano -- 17 bytes, "FOUND GICv3 ITS", then silence. That
# established the defect was not Tegra-specific. But it was SYMPTOM IDENTITY
# ONLY, n=1: no ftrace ever confirmed the a1.metal hang was the same NISV fault
# (results/gicv3-nisv-debug/20260909T101030Z/summary.md, hypothesis H-C).
#
# On 2026-09-18 the defect was cleared at the source on the Orin by rebuilding
# startup-qemu-virt with -fno-auto-inc-dec, from board source written in-repo.
# This run asks whether that fix also works on a second vendor's silicon.
#
# METHOD: the 2026-07-29 launch line, verbatim, changing exactly one thing per
# arm -- which IFS is booted. Control first: if the shipped startup does not
# reproduce the hang here today, the test arm proves nothing and the run is void.
#
#   sudo timeout 60 QEMU -machine virt,gic-version=3 \
#     -cpu host -enable-kvm -smp 2 -m 1G \
#     -drive file=disk-qemu,if=none,id=drv0,format=raw \
#     -device virtio-blk-device,drive=drv0 -kernel <IFS> -nographic \
#     -serial file:<log> -display none -no-reboot -monitor none
#
# QEMU: QEMU emulator version 6.2.0 (Debian 1:6.2+dfsg-2ubuntu6.31)
#
# ARTEFACTS (sha256, verified on this host after transfer):
#   92868b2f116f17c06d26585b9618682076913d17491bdeb22c4a0392d43dd745  ifs.bin
#   26170cd7dc74c216181db71c5da0e6b22719d1f3f9f2c1d0c2b9723fd6c9446c  ifs-kvmfix.bin
#   669345e772d968944d9d9890d89fdb2d8f062506e7e4132c1225597dabb62b2a  disk-qemu
#   ifs.bin        = the SDP's shipped startup  (CONTROL)
#   ifs-kvmfix.bin = startup rebuilt with -fno-auto-inc-dec  (TEST)
#   disk-qemu      = the same disk as the 2026-07-29 run
#
# QEMU_EXIT=124 in both arms means the 60s timeout wrapper killed it, not a
# crash -- the same convention as the 2026-07-29 header.
#
